{%- comment -%}
  The studio's three off-site destinations — Instagram, Shopee, TikTok.

  WHY THIS IS A SNIPPET AND NOT THREE COPIES. The same three links now render in
  three places: the footer's bottom row, the home page and the About page. Three
  renderers reading one source is the whole reason this exists — a studio that
  changes its TikTok handle must not have to remember there are two other places
  it is written down.

  THE URLs MOVED TO THEME SETTINGS ON 2026-09-27, and that is the change worth
  knowing about. They used to be four generic label/URL pairs on the FOOTER
  section, which made sense while the footer was the only thing rendering them.
  It is not any more, and one section's settings are not readable from another
  section. So `settings.social_*_url` in config/settings_schema.json is the one
  source, and the footer reads it like everything else does.

  WHAT WAS GIVEN UP: the old shape let the studio add a marketplace nobody had
  anticipated without a code change, because the pairs were generic. These three
  are named, because each one now draws its own trademarked glyph and a fourth
  generic entry would have nothing to draw. Adding a fourth platform is now a
  settings field plus a mark in `icon.liquid` — more work than before, and the
  price of showing logos at all.

  AN EMPTY URL RENDERS NOTHING AT ALL, per platform. Same rule the footer's link
  columns follow: no dead buttons. With all three blank this outputs nothing and
  `social-band.liquid` drops its own section rather than drawing an empty band.

  THE ORDER IS INSTAGRAM, SHOPEE, TIKTOK — brief §19's own order, and it is not
  alphabetical or arbitrary. Shopee is the studio's actual till and sits in the
  middle where a three-up group is read first.

  Accepts:
    variant - 'cards' for the 3-up panel; anything else renders the footer row
    class   - extra classes on the wrapper
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign ig_url = settings.social_instagram_url
  assign shopee_url = settings.social_shopee_url
  assign tiktok_url = settings.social_tiktok_url
  assign handle = settings.social_handle
-%}

{%- if ig_url != blank or shopee_url != blank or tiktok_url != blank -%}

  {%- if variant == 'cards' -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      THE CARD GRID. `sm:grid-cols-3` rather than a flex row: three cards that
      each carry a mark, a name and a handle need equal widths to read as one
      set, and a wrapping flex row gives the last one a different width the
      moment one platform is missing.

      The mark sits on `bg-surface` — white, the token for a thing ON the page —
      inside a card on `bg-canvas`. That is the three-deep surface stack the
      design system describes, used as intended rather than invented here.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <ul class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3 {{ class }}">
      {%- for entry in (1..3) -%}
        {%- liquid
          case forloop.index
            when 1
              assign social_url = ig_url
              assign social_icon = 'instagram'
              assign social_name = 'Instagram'
            when 2
              assign social_url = shopee_url
              assign social_icon = 'shopee'
              assign social_name = 'Shopee'
            when 3
              assign social_url = tiktok_url
              assign social_icon = 'tiktok'
              assign social_name = 'TikTok'
          endcase
        -%}
        {%- if social_url != blank -%}
          <li>
            {%- comment -%}
              Off-site, so `rel="noopener"` and the screen-reader suffix every
              external link on this site carries. The whole card is the link
              rather than a button inside it: a card that looks clickable and is
              not is the most common thing to get wrong here.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <a
              href="{{ social_url }}"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              class="group flex h-full min-h-11 flex-col items-start gap-4 rounded-md border border-border bg-canvas p-5 transition-colors duration-(--duration-base) hover:border-brand hover:bg-surface lg:p-6"
            >
              <span class="flex size-12 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-surface text-brand transition-colors duration-(--duration-base) group-hover:bg-brand group-hover:text-on-brand">
                {% render 'icon', icon: social_icon, class: 'size-6' %}
              </span>

              <span class="flex flex-col gap-1">
                <span class="font-display text-h4 text-ink">{{ social_name }}</span>
                {%- if handle != blank -%}
                  <span class="text-caption text-ink-muted">{{ handle }}</span>
                {%- endif -%}
              </span>

              <span class="sr-only">{{ 'general.opens_new_tab' | t }}</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    </ul>

  {%- else -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      THE FOOTER ROW. Mark plus word, not mark alone. A bare glyph row would be
      shorter, and it would also be three unlabelled shapes on a brown gradient
      — the site's own rule is that colour and shape never carry meaning alone.
      The word is also what makes Shopee legible to a reader who does not know
      the mark, which on this store is the link that matters most.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <ul class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-2 {{ class }}">
      {%- for entry in (1..3) -%}
        {%- liquid
          case forloop.index
            when 1
              assign social_url = ig_url
              assign social_icon = 'instagram'
              assign social_name = 'Instagram'
            when 2
              assign social_url = shopee_url
              assign social_icon = 'shopee'
              assign social_name = 'Shopee'
            when 3
              assign social_url = tiktok_url
              assign social_icon = 'tiktok'
              assign social_name = 'TikTok'
          endcase
        -%}
        {%- if social_url != blank -%}
          <li>
            {%- comment -%}
              The colour is restated for the reason every other footer link
              restates it: base.css paints every anchor --color-brand, which is
              one of this footer's own gradient stops, so an unstyled link all
              but vanishes into its background. Hover is an underline, not a
              colour change — cream has no brighter neighbour to move toward.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <a
              href="{{ social_url }}"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center gap-2 text-micro uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-invert underline-offset-4 transition-colors duration-(--duration-base) hover:underline"
            >
              {% render 'icon', icon: social_icon, class: 'size-4 shrink-0' %}
              {{ social_name }}
              <span class="sr-only">{{ 'general.opens_new_tab' | t }}</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    </ul>
  {%- endif -%}

{%- endif -%}
